# Phase 2 — make lexical decisions first-class

Once the translation proof is stable, the next major asset is the **lexical/lemma layer**.

You already have pieces of this:

```text
surface form
lemma
morphology witnesses
literal gloss
lexical sense
sense evidence
translation occurrence
```

Now turn them into stable objects.

Conceptually:

```text
Lemma
  ├── form
  ├── root/derivation where appropriate
  ├── morphology evidence
  └── occurrences

LexicalSense
  ├── lemma
  ├── definition
  ├── domain/tradition
  ├── temporal/textual scope
  └── evidence

SenseUse
  ├── passage
  ├── exact token/span
  ├── chosen sense
  ├── alternatives
  ├── rationale
  └── review status
```

Then every translated technical term can resolve.

For `vimarśa`, for example:

```text
vimarśa
├── occurrence IPVV-X
│   ├── selected sense S4
│   ├── alternatives S2/S5
│   ├── Torella evidence
│   ├── Ratié evidence
│   └── reviewer judgment
├── occurrence TĀ-Y
...
```

This creates the eventual **Term Audit / Lemma Tracker** product almost automatically.

The product can show:

* every occurrence;
* morphology;
* translations used;
* sense distribution;
* chronology;
* author/text variation;
* reviewer disagreements;
* sense changes after correction.

This is one of the strongest scholar-facing assets because it becomes a machine-readable historical lexicon derived from actual textual decisions.

