# Phase 4 — audited logical arguments

Now the argument layer becomes much stronger than the old pilot.

Build:

```text
Argument
├── premises
├── conclusion
├── inference applications
├── warrants
├── objections
├── responses
├── assumptions
├── qualifications
├── counterpositions
└── cruxes
```

And keep three distinct notions:

```text
TEXTUAL GROUNDING
What the text actually says.

INFERENTIAL VALIDITY
Whether the conclusion follows from premises under a declared logic/profile.

DIALECTICAL STATUS
Whether objections/defeaters survive.
```

This distinction is essential.

Then the same argument can be run through multiple evaluators:

```text
Pāṭala semantic consistency
Nyāya defect checker
ASPIC defeasible evaluator
formal logic where applicable
philological grounding
scholar evidence
```

So instead of:

> “Argument is valid.”

you might get:

```text
ARG-42

TEXTUAL:
  premise P1 EXACT
  premise P2 RECONSTRUCTED
  conclusion QUALIFIED

INFERENCE:
  modus-like structure VALID under profile X

NYĀYA:
  possible asiddha candidate on P2

DIALECTIC:
  rebuttal R7 defeats warrant W2 unless assumption A3 accepted

SCHOLARSHIP:
  Ratié SUPPORTS P1
  Torella QUALIFIES P2
```

That is an **audited logical argument**.

