# Phase 5 — Crux engine

This becomes one of the most interesting Pāṭala-native capabilities.

A crux is not “controversial sentence.”

It should mean:

> a disputed assumption/proposition/inference whose change materially alters a downstream conclusion.

Given:

```text
P1
P2
P3
 ↓
C
```

run counterfactual evaluation:

```text
remove/flip P1 → C survives
remove/flip P2 → C fails
remove/flip P3 → C survives
```

Then:

```text
P2 = load-bearing crux
```

This produces:

```text
Crux
├── affected argument
├── assumption/proposition
├── competing positions
├── outcome sensitivity
├── source dependencies
├── scholar disagreements
└── downstream impact
```

This is valuable to scholars because it tells them:

> “Here is the exact point on which this interpretation actually turns.”

Much better than endless prose disagreement.

