# The full roadmap compressed into checkpoints

I would formalize the rest of the project as:

```text
P0  Proof-carrying SOURCE→C1 compiler
P1  LexicalSense + SenseUse graph
P2  Canonical Proposition extraction
P3  Audited Argument IR
P4  Crux + counterfactual evaluator
P5  Scholar evidence compiler
P6  Proof-of-Scholarship / ReviewEvent
P7  Executable corrections + ImpactReport
P8  Lemma/Term Intelligence product
P9  Translation Audit + Compare
P10 Adversarial Review + argument explorer
P11 Scholar Workbench
P12 Contributor/reviewer identity + expertise graph
P13 Peer-assessment workflow
P14 Scholar funding / incentives
P15 Trust API / MCP
P16 Standards exports + release packages
P17 Cross-tradition compiler
```

But in terms of **actual execution**, don't work on all of that now.

The next hard sequence is:

```text
NOW
Agent2 finishes canonical SOURCE→C1
Agent1 builds proof for each layer

THEN
SenseUse/Lemma graph

THEN
Propositions + audited arguments

THEN
Scholar evidence attaches to those propositions

THEN
ReviewEvent + correction propagation

THEN
products become projections of that graph
```

That order matters.

The scholar products become easy once the underlying graph contains **addressable terms, claims, arguments, evidence and reviews**. Building their UIs earlier would mostly create empty shells.

So the true convergence is:

> **Machine-verifiable translation establishes the textual substrate. Proposition and argument compilation make the tradition computable. Scholar evidence and typed review make it trustworthy. Dependency-aware correction makes it maintainable. The scholar products are then simply different views and workflows over that same living proof graph.**
